Reason: The decision to open the pause menu depends solely on `packetHandler.isLoadingLevel`, a flag tied to multiplayer network packet handling. In singleplayer, since no real network packets are processed, this flag may never be properly reset to `false`, so `!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` evaluates to `false`, causing `pause()` to never be called and the menu to never appear when Escape is pressed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (Keyboard.getEventKey() == Keyboard.KEY_ESCAPE) {
    if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel || !isOnline()){
        pause();
    } else {
        Mouse.setGrabbed(false);
    }
}
Reason: By adding `|| !isOnline()`, the condition now also opens the pause menu when the game is not in an online/multiplayer session, regardless of the (potentially stale or irrelevant) `isLoadingLevel` flag. This ensures that in singleplayer mode, pressing Escape correctly triggers `pause()` since `isOnline()` is false, bypassing the flawed dependency on network-related state.

